-- glucose, where the thing the setting decides is not how many signals one molecule has but how many
-- molecules there are. In water the pyranose ring opens and closes, and the hydroxyl at the anomeric
-- carbon comes back on either face: alpha and beta D-glucopyranose, interconverting through the open
-- chain, which is less than 0.003% of the solution. At equilibrium in water at 25 C the mixture is about
-- 36% alpha and 64% beta -- the ratio behind the specific rotations, +112 for the alpha anomer, +19 for
-- the beta and +52.7 for the equilibrium solution, which is the measurement mutarotation was discovered
-- by (Dubrunfaut, 1846). The rate is about 0.015 per minute at 26 C, which is 2.5 x 10^-4 per second: an
-- order of magnitude of zero, and far below anything a spectrometer averages. So the two anomeric protons
-- stay separate, a doublet at 5.22 ppm for the alpha anomer and one at 4.64 for the beta, with the
-- integrals in the ratio of the composition -- which is how the anomerization is followed in real time,
-- and how alpha-D-glucose is used as a tracer in vivo.
--
-- the exchange is not an automorphism of a molecule: it changes the configuration at the anomeric carbon,
-- so it is a path between two forms rather than a permutation within one. Modelled here as the
-- interconversion class carrying both forms' anomeric protons, with the exchange as the operation between
-- them, which is what an interconversion class is.
glu-anomers : Compound
glu-anomers = (2 , cons (nat-xch (cons 0 nil) (cons 1 nil) , rate-flat 0 , true) nil , cons 0 (cons 1 nil))
